Q: Cannot search anything A: <p>I tried to run code and first problem is <code>load_data();</code> without value for parameter <code>query</code> so it treats it as <code>undefined</code> and you try to send <code>data:{query:undefined}</code> but <code>ajax()</code> skips <code>data:{query:undefined}</code> and server raises error for <code>request.form['query']</code> beacuse form doesn't have key <code>query</code>.</p>
<p>You would have to use one of method:</p>
<ul>
<li>execute it with empty string <code>load_data('');</code> (in all places)</li>
<li>replace <code>undefined</code> with empty string using <code>||</code> as <code>or</code> - like <code>data:{query:query||''}</code></li>
<li>in Python use <code>form.get('query, '')</code> to get empty string when there is no <code>query</code> in <code>request.form</code></li>
</ul>
<hr />
<p>Another problem can be with <code>numrows</code> which you don't create when <code>search_word == ''</code> but later you have <code>render_template(..., numrows=numrows)</code> and it should raise error.</p>
<hr />
<p>Both problems should raise errors in Python and you should see them in console. They should show you what is wrong with your code. And you should show error messages in question.</p>
<hr />
<p>Other idea: maybe problem is how you use database.</p>
<p>If you don't have <code>conn.row_factory = sqlite3.Row</code> then it may need <code>row[0]</code> instead of <code>row.id</code>, etc. And this may gives empty values in table.</p>
<hr />
<p>Full working code with example data<br />
(it needs to execute <code>create_database()</code> <code>insert_example_data()</code>)<br />
and with HTML directly in code (using <code>render_template_string</code>)<br />
so everyone can simply copy and run it.</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>import sqlite3

from flask import Flask, request, jsonify, render_template, render_template_string

app = Flask(__name__)


def create_database():
    conn = sqlite3.connect(&quot;data.sqlite&quot;)
    cur = conn.cursor()

    cur.execute(
        &quot;&quot;&quot;
    DROP TABLE IF EXISTS users;
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    )

    cur.execute(
        &quot;&quot;&quot;
    CREATE TABLE users (
        id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
        name TEXT NOT NULL,
        email TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,
        role_id INTEGER NOT NULL
    );
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    )

    conn.commit()
    conn.close()

    print(&quot;Database created.&quot;)


def insert_example_data():
    conn = sqlite3.connect(&quot;data.sqlite&quot;)
    cur = conn.cursor()

    users = [
        (&quot;Alice Johnson&quot;, &quot;alice@example.com&quot;, 1),
        (&quot;Bob Smith&quot;, &quot;bob@example.com&quot;, 2),
        (&quot;Charlie Brown&quot;, &quot;charlie@example.com&quot;, 3),
        (&quot;David Miller&quot;, &quot;david@example.com&quot;, 1),
        (&quot;Eva Green&quot;, &quot;eva@example.com&quot;, 2),
        (&quot;Frank Underwood&quot;, &quot;frank@example.com&quot;, 3),
        (&quot;Grace Hopper&quot;, &quot;grace@example.com&quot;, 2),
        (&quot;Hannah Lee&quot;, &quot;hannah@example.com&quot;, 1),
    ]

    cur.executemany(
        &quot;&quot;&quot;
    INSERT INTO users (name, email, role_id)
    VALUES (?, ?, ?)
    &quot;&quot;&quot;,
        users,
    )

    conn.commit()
    conn.close()

    print(&quot;Seed data inserted.&quot;)


def get_db_connection():
    conn = sqlite3.connect(&quot;data.sqlite&quot;)
    conn.row_factory = sqlite3.Row
    cur = conn.cursor()
    return cur


